---
import Split from './Split.astro';
import Hd from './Hd.astro';
---
<Split alt="Image placeholder" placeholder="[Image placeholder]" narrow>
<Fragment slot="title">Better substitutions</Fragment>
<Fragment slot="lead">When the product isn’t on the shelf</Fragment>
<p class="sans">Client: S Group / S-kaupat · Timeframe: May–June 2026 · Type: Discovery · Status: In progress</p>
<p>S-kaupat is S Group’s online grocery service. Every order carries a quiet risk: by the time the picker reaches the shelf, the product the customer ordered may not be there.</p>
<p>S-kaupat wanted to find new ways to substitute better, both when a product is actually out of stock and when the data already predicts it won’t be available on the delivery day.</p>
<p><b>I led the discovery end to end.</b> I planned and facilitated all three workshops, analysed the availability data, synthesised the findings into root causes and a prioritised roadmap, and designed the first concepts.</p>
</Split>

<section class="fold fold2">
<div class="top">
<div class="top-l">
<h2 class="h">Project approach</h2>
<p class="sans lead" style="max-width: none">A three-workshop discovery that moved from data, through employees’ experience, to the end customer. I designed the process and the workshop materials, facilitated every session and turned the outputs into the final synthesis.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="tl">
<div class="group g-1">
<div class="tagline"><span class="tag">MAY 2026</span><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="items c1">
<div class="step serif"><span class="num">1.</span><div class="step-body"><span><b>Workshop 1: Data.</b> We analysed availability and substitution data. Participants answered pre-work questions on known challenges, existing concepts and their own goals.</span></div></div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="group g-2">
<div class="tagline"><span class="tag">JUNE 2026</span><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="items c2">
<div class="step serif"><span class="num">2.</span><div class="step-body"><span><b>Workshop 2: Employee view.</b> We clustered the findings into themes, wrote service statements from the picker’s point of view and ideated solutions from problem and opportunity statements.</span></div></div>
<div class="step serif"><span class="num">3.</span><div class="step-body"><span><b>Workshop 3: Customer view.</b> Same process, but through the end customer’s eyes, ending in Crazy Eights sketching.</span></div></div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="group g-1">
<div class="tagline"><span class="tag">AUG 2026 →</span><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="items c1">
<div class="step serif"><span class="num">4.</span><div class="step-body"><span><b>Concepting and testing.</b> The strongest hypotheses are now being turned into concepts and validated.</span></div></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="fold">
<Hd>
<Fragment slot="title">Three root causes</Fragment>
The availability and substitution problem wasn’t one broken thing. It was three that made each other worse.
</Hd>
<div class="rowpad">
<div class="items c3">
<div class="step serif"><span class="num">1.</span><div class="step-body"><span><b>Availability data can’t be fully trusted.</b> Stock levels, shelving rhythm, assortment changes and forecasts don’t always match reality at picking time. The result is unnecessary shortages and substitutions, and customers who lose trust when the delivery doesn’t match the basket.</span></div></div>
<div class="step serif"><span class="num">2.</span><div class="step-body"><span><b>Communication doesn’t make uncertainty manageable.</b> Information is spread across channels, and there’s no single place to see what happened to an order. When customers don’t understand what’s going on, uncertainty turns into distrust.</span></div></div>
<div class="step serif"><span class="num">3.</span><div class="step-body"><span><b>Decision support is too weak for both customer and picker.</b> Customers can’t set their own substitution rules, so pickers make judgement calls without personalised guidance. Substitution quality varies and picking slows down.</span></div></div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="rowpad">
<p class="h quote">“The customer is a bystander in their own order.”</p>
</div>
</section>

<section class="fold">
<Hd>
<Fragment slot="title">Two hypotheses that guided the work</Fragment>
The goal isn’t to make every availability problem disappear. It’s to make those problems understandable and manageable for everyone involved.
</Hd>
<div class="cols2">
<div class="col">
<h3>Picker</h3>
<div class="tagline"><span class="tag">Hypothesis</span><span class="line"></span></div>
<p class="strong">When availability data is up to date and substitution logic is clear, pickers can decide quickly, without guesswork or interruptions.</p>
</div>
<div class="col">
<h3>Customer</h3>
<div class="tagline"><span class="tag">Hypothesis</span><span class="line"></span></div>
<p class="strong">When the customer’s choices, real-time information and automated decisions come together in clear communication, the customer always knows what to expect.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="fold">
<Hd>
<Fragment slot="title">From insight to priorities</Fragment>
These were followed by picker decision support, probability-based availability messaging, and a dynamic interface that steers customers to better delivery slots.
</Hd>
<div class="rowpad">
<div class="group">
<div class="tagline"><span class="tag">Priorities</span><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="items c3">
<div class="step serif"><span class="num">1.</span><div class="step-body"><span><b>Fix the availability data.</b> Without reliable data, better communication or picker support can’t deliver real change.</span></div></div>
<div class="step serif"><span class="num">2.</span><div class="step-body"><span><b>Real-time communication with the customer.</b> Approve, edit and give feedback on substitutions during and after picking. The customer takes part in the order instead of being surprised by it.</span></div></div>
<div class="step serif"><span class="num">3.</span><div class="step-body"><span><b>Customer-defined substitution rules.</b> Substitute, don’t substitute, or only a specific product, plus price tolerance and allergy and dislike profiles. Substitution becomes a designed part of the service rather than something that feels like an error.</span></div></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<Split alt="Image placeholder: two phone mockups" placeholder="[Image placeholder: two phone mockups]" pw={58.9} rev padR narrow>
<Fragment slot="title">First concepts</Fragment>
<Fragment slot="lead"><b>A clear order view.</b> It shows what was promised, what will be delivered and what was substituted, and lets the customer rate each substitution with a quick 👍/👎.</Fragment>
<p><b>Availability alerts in the basket.</b> Well-known products with limited availability are flagged while the customer shops, with a nudge to switch to a time slot where they’re more likely to be in stock.</p>
</Split>

<section class="fold">
<Hd>
<Fragment slot="title">Where we are now</Fragment>
The discovery is complete, and the work has moved into concepting and testing.
</Hd>
